course: dictionary
lesson: 9169
title: Snowy: two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Snowy has two meanings, and the words around it tell you which one you need. The first meaning is having a lot of snow, or with snow falling. «High in the snowy mountain forests of central China, a troop of monkeys huddles together in the snow.» Mountains and forests with snow on them are snowy, so this is the first meaning.
explain: The second meaning is white like snow. Look at «a snowy owl» or «snowy hair». Nothing in those phrases is about weather, so snowy must be describing colour. Ask yourself: what is being described, and what could it look like?
explain: Snowy is an adjective, so it describes a noun. The noun next to it gives you the clue: snowy weather, snowy roads, snowy hair, snowy owl. Try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Snowy is an adjective, so it describes a noun, such as snowy mountains.
fact: «Snowy» means having a lot of snow or with snow falling.
fact: «Snowy» also means white like snow, as in «snowy hair» or «a snowy owl».
fact: Snowy roads and paths can be slippery, so take care when you walk on them.
fact: The comparative forms are snowier and snowiest.
exercise: In «High in the snowy mountain forests of central China», what does snowy mean? | *Having a lot of snow | Very tall | Covered in mud | Very cold and windy
exercise: Poppy has a bunny called Snowy. What colour does the name suggest? | *White like snow | Green | Black | Orange
exercise: «Last winter was snowy, but this winter is snowier still.» Which word is the comparative form of snowy? | still | winter | snowy | *snowier
test: Which is most likely to be slippery? | A dry playground | *A snowy road | A pencil case | A clean window
test: In «a snowy owl», what does snowy most likely mean? | Very quick | Brown and spotted | Wet and muddy | *White like snow
exam: Which sentence uses snowy to mean white like snow? | *«Her snowy hair shone under the lamp.» | «The snowy mountains were empty.» | «Snowy weather is forecast for Friday.» | «A snowy road is slippery.»
